{"entries":[{"title":"Untitled","url":"/about/index.html","content":"Skip to main content Skip to content FeaturesPostsExplore by tagContact Us 🇬🇧 English 🇫🇷 Français 🇸🇦 العربية 🇧🇩 বাংলা 🇨🇿 Čeština 🇩🇪 Deutsch 🇪🇸 Español 🇳🇬 Hausa 🇮🇱 עברית 🇮🇳 हिन्दी 🇮🇩 Indonesia 🇮🇹 Italiano 🇯🇵 日本語 🇰🇷 한국어 🇳🇱 Nederlands 🇵🇱 Polski 🇵🇹 Português 🇷🇴 Română 🇷🇺 Русский 🇸🇪 Svenska 🇹🇭 ไทย 🇵🇭 Filipino 🇹🇷 Türkçe 🇺🇦 Українська 🇻🇳 Tiếng Việt 🇳🇬 Yorùbá 🇨🇳 简体中文 🇹🇼 繁體中文 Home>About About About I trained as a cognitive psychologist at the University of Lagos. In 2018 I moved into UX research at a London charity. I have been independent since 2022. I write findings the way researchers expect. That means clear methods, honest sample sizes, and no vague \"users said\" claims. Clients say this makes the difference between a report people read and one that gathers dust. How I work Discovery first. Every project starts with a paid one-week discovery. That way we both know what we are getting into. In writing, not slides. Every finding lives in a written document with citations, not a deck that fades after the readout. Fortnightly check-ins. A 30-minute call every other Wednesday, even on long projects, so nothing drifts. No NDAs that bury bad findings. I will sign fair privacy terms. I will not sign clauses that stop me from flagging problems. Speaking and writing I write now and then for A List Apart. I have spoken at UX London, Service Design in Government, and the Interaction conference. Slides and notes are on request. Education MSc Cognitive Psychology, University of Lagos (2017) BSc Psychology, Obafemi Awolowo University (2015) Currently reading Articulating Design Decisions — Tom Greever Interviewing Users (2nd ed.) — Steve Portigal Just Enough Research — Erika Hall","headings":["About","About","How I work","Speaking and writing","Education","Currently reading"]},{"title":"Untitled","url":"/contact/index.html","content":"Skip to main content Skip to content FeaturesPostsExplore by tagContact Us 🇬🇧 English 🇫🇷 Français 🇸🇦 العربية 🇧🇩 বাংলা 🇨🇿 Čeština 🇩🇪 Deutsch 🇪🇸 Español 🇳🇬 Hausa 🇮🇱 עברית 🇮🇳 हिन्दी 🇮🇩 Indonesia 🇮🇹 Italiano 🇯🇵 日本語 🇰🇷 한국어 🇳🇱 Nederlands 🇵🇱 Polski 🇵🇹 Português 🇷🇴 Română 🇷🇺 Русский 🇸🇪 Svenska 🇹🇭 ไทย 🇵🇭 Filipino 🇹🇷 Türkçe 🇺🇦 Українська 🇻🇳 Tiếng Việt 🇳🇬 Yorùbá 🇨🇳 简体中文 🇹🇼 繁體中文 Home>Contact Contact Contact Email jane@example.com to get in touch. We'd love to hear from you. We aim to reply within two days.","headings":["Contact","Contact"]},{"title":"Untitled","url":"/field-notes-collective/index.html","content":"Skip to main content Skip to content FeaturesPostsExplore by tagContact Us 🇬🇧 English 🇫🇷 Français 🇸🇦 العربية 🇧🇩 বাংলা 🇨🇿 Čeština 🇩🇪 Deutsch 🇪🇸 Español 🇳🇬 Hausa 🇮🇱 עברית 🇮🇳 हिन्दी 🇮🇩 Indonesia 🇮🇹 Italiano 🇯🇵 日本語 🇰🇷 한국어 🇳🇱 Nederlands 🇵🇱 Polski 🇵🇹 Português 🇷🇴 Română 🇷🇺 Русский 🇸🇪 Svenska 🇹🇭 ไทย 🇵🇭 Filipino 🇹🇷 Türkçe 🇺🇦 Українська 🇻🇳 Tiếng Việt 🇳🇬 Yorùbá 🇨🇳 简体中文 🇹🇼 繁體中文 Home>Field Notes Collective Field Notes Collective At a glance Client: Field Notes Collective (environmental research nonprofit) Year: 2025 Role: Lead researcher and information architect Duration: 6 weeks Team: Solo, with weekly check-ins to FNC's communications lead The problem FNC had 14 years of field research locked in Dropbox folders. This included interviews, soil sample reports, and climate logs. New researchers could not find prior work. Old researchers had no confidence their work would stay findable. They had a budget for \"a website.\" But they did not know if they needed a CMS, a static archive, or something else. What I did Week 1 — Discovery. Twelve 45-minute interviews with current and former field researchers in Nigeria, Kenya, and Ghana. The pattern was clear: people needed to find work by location and topic, not by author or date. The old folder layout made both impossible. Week 2 — IA design. Card-sorted 200 sample documents with five people. Chose a two-axis system: ecological zone (12 types) crossed with topic (8 types). A tree test showed task success rose from 32% to 89%. Weeks 3-5 — Build. Worked with FNC's part-time developer to move documents into a static-site archive. Added full-text search and per-axis filtering. Week 6 — Handover. Wrote a guide for adding new documents. Trained two staff members in 90 minutes each. Outcome Search-to-find time dropped from 11 minutes to 47 seconds (same 8 tasks, before and after) The two trained staff have added 36 new documents in four months with no support questions FNC's funder cited the archive as a key factor in renewing their three-year grant","headings":["Field Notes Collective","At a glance","The problem","What I did","Outcome"]},{"title":"Untitled","url":"/index.html","content":"Skip to main content Skip to content FeaturesPostsExplore by tagContact Us🇬🇧 English 🇫🇷 Français 🇸🇦 العربية 🇧🇩 বাংলা 🇨🇿 Čeština 🇩🇪 Deutsch 🇪🇸 Español 🇳🇬 Hausa 🇮🇱 עברית 🇮🇳 हिन्दी 🇮🇩 Indonesia 🇮🇹 Italiano 🇯🇵 日本語 🇰🇷 한국어 🇳🇱 Nederlands 🇵🇱 Polski 🇵🇹 Português 🇷🇴 Română 🇷🇺 Русский 🇸🇪 Svenska 🇹🇭 ไทย 🇵🇭 Filipino 🇹🇷 Türkçe 🇺🇦 Українська 🇻🇳 Tiếng Việt 🇳🇬 Yorùbá 🇨🇳 简体中文 🇹🇼 繁體中文Maya OkaforIndependent UX research and product design — for founders, charities, and small product teams.Get Started Learn MoreUX research and product designHello — I'm Maya Okafor, a freelance UX researcher and designer. I'm based in Lagos and London.I work with founders, charities, and small teams. They need good research without a long contract. Most projects run two to six weeks. Some clients keep me on a weekly retainer.Selected workField Notes Collective (2025) — Archive rebuild for a nonprofit. 12 interviews, full IA rework, shipped in 6 weeks.Linden Editions (2024) — Editorial site for a literary journal. Print-to-web type that reads like a magazine.Polaris Maps (2024) — Wayfinding for a map publisher. A 4-month build with the cartography team.See all projects or how I work.What I offerDiscovery research — interviews, diary studies, field workUsability testing — moderated and unmoderated, with clear reportsInfo architecture — card sorts, tree tests, sitemap designDesign system audits — for teams whose UI is ahead of their docsWhat I don't offerVisual rebrands, marketing redesigns, or AI roadmap work. Great people do those things. I'm happy to point you to them.Get in touchEmail hello@mayaokafor.studio. I reply within two business days. Projects start with a paid one-week discovery.","headings":["Maya Okafor","UX research and product design","Selected work","What I offer","What I don't offer","Get in touch"]},{"title":"Untitled","url":"/linden-editions/index.html","content":"Skip to main content Skip to content FeaturesPostsExplore by tagContact Us 🇬🇧 English 🇫🇷 Français 🇸🇦 العربية 🇧🇩 বাংলা 🇨🇿 Čeština 🇩🇪 Deutsch 🇪🇸 Español 🇳🇬 Hausa 🇮🇱 עברית 🇮🇳 हिन्दी 🇮🇩 Indonesia 🇮🇹 Italiano 🇯🇵 日本語 🇰🇷 한국어 🇳🇱 Nederlands 🇵🇱 Polski 🇵🇹 Português 🇷🇴 Română 🇷🇺 Русский 🇸🇪 Svenska 🇹🇭 ไทย 🇵🇭 Filipino 🇹🇷 Türkçe 🇺🇦 Українська 🇻🇳 Tiếng Việt 🇳🇬 Yorùbá 🇨🇳 简体中文 🇹🇼 繁體中文 Home>Linden Editions Linden Editions At a glance Client: Linden Editions (independent literary journal) Year: 2024 Role: Design lead Duration: 10 weeks Team: Me + 1 developer (the journal's editor) The problem Linden had been publishing a print quarterly for six years. Their existing website was a WordPress theme that displayed posts in reverse chronological order — fine for a blog, wrong for a journal that wanted readers to browse issues, the way you would in a library. What I did Audit. Read all 24 back issues. Catalogued the recurring sections (essay, fiction, poetry, review, interview), the typographic conventions (drop caps, em-dash conversations, full-bleed photography), and the editorial voice (long-form, slow, deliberate). Type system. Built a four-face type system: Newzald for body, Söhne Mono for footnotes, Tiempos for display, Reckless for poetry. All four sit on a shared 24px baseline. The system has 11 tokens — small enough that the editor maintains it herself. IA. Issues, not posts. The home page is a stack of issue covers. Each issue has its own permalink and table of contents. Individual pieces are nested under their issue, never floating loose. Build. A static site (no CMS) with a markdown workflow the editor manages from her local machine. Total page weight: 38 KB on the home page, 84 KB on a typical essay. No JavaScript on the reading experience. Outcome Average session length doubled (1m 12s → 2m 41s) in the four months after launch The journal's print-to-digital subscription conversion went from 4% to 11% Editor reports the markdown workflow takes \"about 20 minutes per issue, where the old WordPress backend took an afternoon\"","headings":["Linden Editions","At a glance","The problem","What I did","Outcome"]},{"title":"Untitled","url":"/polaris-maps/index.html","content":"Skip to main content Skip to content FeaturesPostsExplore by tagContact Us 🇬🇧 English 🇫🇷 Français 🇸🇦 العربية 🇧🇩 বাংলা 🇨🇿 Čeština 🇩🇪 Deutsch 🇪🇸 Español 🇳🇬 Hausa 🇮🇱 עברית 🇮🇳 हिन्दी 🇮🇩 Indonesia 🇮🇹 Italiano 🇯🇵 日本語 🇰🇷 한국어 🇳🇱 Nederlands 🇵🇱 Polski 🇵🇹 Português 🇷🇴 Română 🇷🇺 Русский 🇸🇪 Svenska 🇹🇭 ไทย 🇵🇭 Filipino 🇹🇷 Türkçe 🇺🇦 Українська 🇻🇳 Tiếng Việt 🇳🇬 Yorùbá 🇨🇳 简体中文 🇹🇼 繁體中文 Home>Polaris Maps Polaris Maps At a glance Client: Polaris Maps (independent cartographic publisher) Year: 2024 Role: Design researcher and wayfinding designer Duration: 16 weeks (4-month engagement, 3 days/week) Team: Me + 2 in-house cartographers + 1 print production lead The problem Polaris publishes city walking maps for tourism boards. Each map has its own visual style set by the destination. But customers (tourism boards, hotel chains) wanted the same wayfinding symbols across every map. They needed one shared set for \"viewpoint,\" \"step-free entrance,\" \"drinking water,\" and more. What I did Field research. Spent 4 days walking with map users in Edinburgh, Bath, and Galway. Noted where they paused, checked the legend, or asked someone for help. In total: 312 wayfinding choices across 23 walks. Symbol audit. Listed every wayfinding symbol Polaris had ever used (218 total). Cut them to 47 key symbols. Of those, 34 were already used the same way in over 70% of maps. The other 13 were the design problem. Symbol design. Worked with the cartography team for 8 weeks. We tested each symbol with print proofs and user tests. The final set scored 93% recognition with 24 new users, up from 64%. Documentation. Wrote a 28-page wayfinding standard that Polaris now ships to every new design team. It is light on theory and full of examples. It shows the rejected versions of every symbol with a note on what failed. Outcome Standard adopted on all new Polaris maps since launch (14 maps in 8 months) Tourist info centres in Edinburgh and Bath report fewer \"where is X\" questions thanks to clearer symbols Polaris won a 2025 European Cartographic Award for the wayfinding standard","headings":["Polaris Maps","At a glance","The problem","What I did","Outcome"]},{"title":"Untitled","url":"/posts/index.html","content":"Skip to main content Skip to content FeaturesPostsExplore by tagContact Us 🇬🇧 English 🇫🇷 Français 🇸🇦 العربية 🇧🇩 বাংলা 🇨🇿 Čeština 🇩🇪 Deutsch 🇪🇸 Español 🇳🇬 Hausa 🇮🇱 עברית 🇮🇳 हिन्दी 🇮🇩 Indonesia 🇮🇹 Italiano 🇯🇵 日本語 🇰🇷 한국어 🇳🇱 Nederlands 🇵🇱 Polski 🇵🇹 Português 🇷🇴 Română 🇷🇺 Русский 🇸🇪 Svenska 🇹🇭 ไทย 🇵🇭 Filipino 🇹🇷 Türkçe 🇺🇦 Українська 🇻🇳 Tiếng Việt 🇳🇬 Yorùbá 🇨🇳 简体中文 🇹🇼 繁體中文 Home>Posts Posts Posts Recent posts and writing.","headings":["Posts","Posts"]},{"title":"Untitled","url":"/privacy/index.html","content":"Skip to main content Skip to content FeaturesPostsExplore by tagContact Us 🇬🇧 English 🇫🇷 Français 🇸🇦 العربية 🇧🇩 বাংলা 🇨🇿 Čeština 🇩🇪 Deutsch 🇪🇸 Español 🇳🇬 Hausa 🇮🇱 עברית 🇮🇳 हिन्दी 🇮🇩 Indonesia 🇮🇹 Italiano 🇯🇵 日本語 🇰🇷 한국어 🇳🇱 Nederlands 🇵🇱 Polski 🇵🇹 Português 🇷🇴 Română 🇷🇺 Русский 🇸🇪 Svenska 🇹🇭 ไทย 🇵🇭 Filipino 🇹🇷 Türkçe 🇺🇦 Українська 🇻🇳 Tiếng Việt 🇳🇬 Yorùbá 🇨🇳 简体中文 🇹🇼 繁體中文 Home>Privacy Privacy Privacy No personal data is collected on this site.","headings":["Privacy","Privacy"]},{"title":"Untitled","url":"/projects/index.html","content":"Skip to main content Skip to content FeaturesPostsExplore by tagContact Us 🇬🇧 English 🇫🇷 Français 🇸🇦 العربية 🇧🇩 বাংলা 🇨🇿 Čeština 🇩🇪 Deutsch 🇪🇸 Español 🇳🇬 Hausa 🇮🇱 עברית 🇮🇳 हिन्दी 🇮🇩 Indonesia 🇮🇹 Italiano 🇯🇵 日本語 🇰🇷 한국어 🇳🇱 Nederlands 🇵🇱 Polski 🇵🇹 Português 🇷🇴 Română 🇷🇺 Русский 🇸🇪 Svenska 🇹🇭 ไทย 🇵🇭 Filipino 🇹🇷 Türkçe 🇺🇦 Українська 🇻🇳 Tiếng Việt 🇳🇬 Yorùbá 🇨🇳 简体中文 🇹🇼 繁體中文 Home>Projects Projects All projects Field Notes Collective — Archive rebuild for a nonprofit. 12 interviews and full IA rework. Shipped in 6 weeks. Linden Editions — Editorial site for a literary journal. Print-to-web type. Four issues live. Polaris Maps — Wayfinding for a map publisher. Built with the cartography team over 16 weeks. Back to home · About me · Get in touch","headings":["Projects","All projects"]},{"title":"Untitled","url":"/tags/index.html","content":"Skip to main content Skip to content FeaturesPostsExplore by tagContact Us 🇬🇧 English 🇫🇷 Français 🇸🇦 العربية 🇧🇩 বাংলা 🇨🇿 Čeština 🇩🇪 Deutsch 🇪🇸 Español 🇳🇬 Hausa 🇮🇱 עברית 🇮🇳 हिन्दी 🇮🇩 Indonesia 🇮🇹 Italiano 🇯🇵 日本語 🇰🇷 한국어 🇳🇱 Nederlands 🇵🇱 Polski 🇵🇹 Português 🇷🇴 Română 🇷🇺 Русский 🇸🇪 Svenska 🇹🇭 ไทย 🇵🇭 Filipino 🇹🇷 Türkçe 🇺🇦 Українська 🇻🇳 Tiếng Việt 🇳🇬 Yorùbá 🇨🇳 简体中文 🇹🇼 繁體中文 Home>Explore by Tag Explore by Tag Explore by Tag Browse all content organised by topic. Featured Tags (27) GDPR (3 Posts) April 15, 2026: Contact - Contact Maya Okafor April 15, 2026: Posts - Blog posts April 15, 2026: Privacy - Privacy policy IA (1 Posts) April 16, 2026: Field Notes Collective - Research archive rebuild for an environmental nonprofit — 12 field interviews, complete IA rework, 6-week ship. Accessibility (1 Posts) April 15, 2026: Explore by Tag - Browse portfolio content by tag Cartography (1 Posts) April 16, 2026: Polaris Maps - Wayfinding system for an independent map publisher — co-designed with the cartography team across 16 weeks. Editorial (1 Posts) April 16, 2026: Linden Editions - Editorial site for a quarterly literary journal — print-to-web typography system, four issues live. Example (3 Posts) April 16, 2026: About - About Maya Okafor — independent UX researcher and product designer. April 16, 2026: Maya Okafor - Independent UX research and product design — Maya Okafor. April 16, 2026: Projects - All projects by Maya Okafor. Nonprofit (1 Posts) April 16, 2026: Field Notes Collective - Research archive rebuild for an environmental nonprofit — 12 field interviews, complete IA rework, 6-week ship. Portfolio (4 Posts) April 16, 2026: About - About Maya Okafor — independent UX researcher and product designer. April 16, 2026: Maya Okafor - Independent UX research and product design — Maya Okafor. April 16, 2026: Projects - All projects by Maya Okafor. April 15, 2026: Explore by Tag - Browse portfolio content by tag Privacy (3 Posts) April 15, 2026: Contact - Contact Maya Okafor April 15, 2026: Posts - Blog posts April 15, 2026: Privacy - Privacy policy Projects (1 Posts) April 15, 2026: Explore by Tag - Browse portfolio content by tag Research (1 Posts) April 16, 2026: Field Notes Collective - Research archive rebuild for an environmental nonprofit — 12 field interviews, complete IA rework, 6-week ship. Rust (1 Posts) April 15, 2026: Explore by Tag - Browse portfolio content by tag Ssg (3 Posts) April 16, 2026: About - About Maya Okafor — independent UX researcher and product designer. April 16, 2026: Maya Okafor - Independent UX research and product design — Maya Okafor. April 16, 2026: Projects - All projects by Maya Okafor. Typography (1 Posts) April 16, 2026: Linden Editions - Editorial site for a quarterly literary journal — print-to-web typography system, four issues live. Wayfinding (1 Posts) April 16, 2026: Polaris Maps - Wayfinding system for an independent map publisher — co-designed with the cartography team across 16 weeks. Webassembly (1 Posts) April 15, 2026: Explore by Tag - Browse portfolio content by tag","headings":["Explore by Tag","Explore by Tag","Featured Tags (27)","GDPR (3 Posts)","IA (1 Posts)","Accessibility (1 Posts)","Cartography (1 Posts)","Editorial (1 Posts)","Example (3 Posts)","Nonprofit (1 Posts)","Portfolio (4 Posts)","Privacy (3 Posts)","Projects (1 Posts)","Research (1 Posts)","Rust (1 Posts)","Ssg (3 Posts)","Typography (1 Posts)","Wayfinding (1 Posts)","Webassembly (1 Posts)"]}]}
